                            Preamble

  The GNU General Public License is a free, copyleft license for
software and other kinds of works, designed to ensure that, to the
best of our ability, users can enjoy the freedoms listed at the
start of this document.  Our design is shaped by years of experience
to fight what we have seen is one of the most frequent ways that
software is made proprietary—through indirect means.  The long and
elaborate measures are not a symptom of bad intentions; they are a
reaction to sustained measures that have been used for decades to
restrict users' freedom.

  With the GNU General Public License, users of a program have the
freedom to run it for any purpose, to study how it works, to adapt
it to their needs, to share copies with others, and to publish
modified versions—provided they honor the conditions that protect
users' freedom: they must provide the source code, preserve rights
notices, and not use the program to restrict others' freedoms.

  This license is designed to protect those freedoms for all users,
including in distributed or networked applications, by requiring
that any distributed or publicly accessible version must carry the
same freedoms.

