  0. Definitions.

  "This License" refers to version 3 of the GNU General Public License.

  "Copyright" also means copyright-like laws that apply to other kinds
of works, such as semiconductor masks.

  "The Program" refers to any copyrightable work licensed under this
License.

  "You" (or "your") means any individual or legal entity exercising
rights under this License.  For legal entities, "you" includes any
entity that controls, is controlled by, or is under common control
with you, but does not include independent entities.

  "Source" form means the preferred form for making modifications,
including software source code, definition files, configuration
scripts, and documentation describing how to build and install.

  "Object" form means any form other than Source, such as compiled
code, generated documentation, or compressed archives.

  A "Standard Interface" means an interface that either is an official
standard defined by a recognized standards body, or, in the case of
interfaces specified for a particular programming language, one that
is widely used among developers working in that language.

  The "System Libraries" of an executable work include, other than
the work as a whole, anything other than the work that (a) is included
in the normal form of packaging a Major Component, and (b) serves
only to enable use of the work with that Major Component, or to
implement a Standard Interface for which an implementation is
available to the public in source code form.

  A "Major Component", in this context, means a major essential
component (kernel, window system, and so on) of the specific
operating system (if any) on which the executable work runs, or a
compiler used to produce the work, or an object code interpreter
used to run it.

  "The Corresponding Source" for a work means all source code needed
to generate or reproduce the work—whether in Source or Object form.

  1. Source Code.

     The source code for a work is defined as above.  For executable
works, the source code is all the source needed to generate the exact
binary—including scripts controlling compilation and installation,
plus interface definitions for libraries used.

